Li","initials":"L"},{"fullName":"Wanderer JP","lastName":"Wanderer","firstName":"Jonathan P","initials":"JP"},{"fullName":"Ehrenfeld JM","lastName":"Ehrenfeld","firstName":"Jesse M","initials":"JM"},{"fullName":"Shaw AD","lastName":"Shaw","firstName":"Andrew D","initials":"AD"},{"fullName":"Hernandez A","lastName":"Hernandez","firstName":"Antonio","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Kumar AB","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Avinash B","initials":"AB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3970-1974"}},{"fullName":"Self WH","lastName":"Self","firstName":"Wesley H","initials":"WH"},{"fullName":"Siew ED","lastName":"Siew","firstName":"Edward D","initials":"ED"},{"fullName":"Dunlap DF","lastName":"Dunlap","firstName":"Debra F","initials":"DF"},{"fullName":"Stollings JL","lastName":"Stollings","firstName":"Joanna L","initials":"JL"},{"fullName":"Sullivan M","lastName":"Sullivan","firstName":"Mark","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Knostman M","lastName":"Knostman","firstName":"Molly","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Mulherin DP","lastName":"Mulherin","firstName":"David P","initials":"DP"},{"fullName":"Hargrove FR","lastName":"Hargrove","firstName":"Fred R","initials":"FR"},{"fullName":"Janz DR","lastName":"Janz","firstName":"David R","initials":"DR"},{"fullName":"Strawbridge S","lastName":"Strawbridge","firstName":"Seth","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2965-297X"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0566-8005"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3119-6873"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4721-3068"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7136-5408"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7664-8263"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9300-3045"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1343-8784"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2965-297X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3427-0140"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3970-1974"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","recommended_reviews","wikipedia","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"195","journalIssueId":2557335,"dateOfPublication":"2017 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2017,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Am J Respir Crit Care Med","title":"American journal of respiratory and critical care medicine","essn":"1535-4970","issn":"1073-449X","isoabbreviation":"Am J Respir Crit Care Med","nlmid":"9421642"},"printPublicationDate":"2017-05-01"},"pubYear":"2017","pageInfo":"1362-1372","abstractText":"<h4>Rationale</h4>Saline is the intravenous fluid most commonly administered to critically ill adults, but it may be associated with acute kidney injury and death. Whether use of balanced crystalloids rather than saline affects patient outcomes remains unknown.<h4>Objectives</h4>To pilot a cluster-randomized, multiple-crossover trial using software tools within the electronic health record to compare saline to balanced crystalloids.<h4>Methods</h4>This was a cluster-randomized, multiple-crossover trial among 974 adults admitted to a tertiary medical intensive care unit from February 3, 2015 to May 31, 2015. The intravenous crystalloid used in the unit alternated monthly between saline (0.9% sodium chloride) and balanced crystalloids (lactated Ringer's solution or Plasma-Lyte A). Enrollment, fluid delivery, and data collection were performed using software tools within the electronic health record. The primary outcome was the difference between study groups in the proportion of isotonic crystalloid administered that was saline. The secondary outcome was major adverse kidney events within 30 days (MAKE30), a composite of death, dialysis, or persistent renal dysfunction.<h4>Measurements and main results</h4>Patients assigned to saline (n = 454) and balanced crystalloids (n = 520) were similar at baseline and received similar volumes of crystalloid by 30 days (median [interquartile range]: 1,424 ml [500-3,377] vs. 1,617 ml [500-3,628]; P = 0.40). Saline made up a larger proportion of the isotonic crystalloid given in the saline group than in the balanced crystalloid group (91% vs. 21%; P < 0.001). MAKE30 did not differ between groups (24.7% vs. 24.6%; P = 0.98).<h4>Conclusions</h4>An electronic health record-embedded, cluster-randomized, multiple-crossover trial comparing saline with balanced crystalloids can produce well-balanced study groups and separation in crystalloid receipt. Clinical trial registered with www.clinicaltrials.gov (NCT 02345486).","affiliation":"1 Division of Allergy, Pulmonary, and Critical Care Medicine.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","research-article","Randomized Controlled Trial","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"HSRD VA","acronym":"HX","grantId":"IIR 13-073","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIGMS NIH HHS","acronym":"GM","grantId":"K23 GM110469","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCATS NIH HHS","acronym":"TR","grantId":"UL1 TR000445","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"T32 HL087738","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NHLBI NIH HHS","acronym":"HL","grantId":"R34 HL105869","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Critical Illness","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sodium Chloride","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Isotonic Solutions","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Fluid Therapy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Critical Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic